  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>As we move down the group from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Li</mi></math>&nbsp;to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Cs</mi></math>, both the atomic size and atomic mass increase. But the increase in atomic mass more than compensates the bigger atomic size. As a result, the densities of alkali metals increase from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Li</mi></math>&nbsp;to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Cs</mi></math>. Potassium is, however, lighter than sodium.</p><p>Hence, the increasing order of density is as given below:</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Li</mi><mo>&#60;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mo>&#60;</mo><mi>Na</mi><mo>&#60;</mo><mi>Rb</mi><mo>&#60;</mo><mi>Cs</mi></math></p><p>&nbsp;</p></blockquote>",
